<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CAPACITOR_DEBUG</key>
	<string>$(CAPACITOR_DEBUG)</string>
	<!-- ProMotion. Without this key iOS caps an app's frame rate at 60 Hz on 120 Hz iPhones
	     (Erik's 16 Pro) — Safari scrolls at 120, a WKWebView inside a third-party app does not
	     unless the app opts in. Scroll, momentum and the glass dock's motion get the full rate. -->
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>North</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>North can use Bluetooth to talk to a supported card reader. Tap to Pay on iPhone itself needs no reader — the phone is the reader.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>North uses the camera so you can photograph job sites, materials, receipts and documents and attach them to the job.</string>
	<!-- THE FENCE WITH THE SCREEN OFF (field feedback 10-13, item 4; Chris 2026-10-14: "use geo tracking
	     to remember when I left jobsite"). Always-location is asked for ONE thing: a circle around the
	     job you are clocked in on, so the phone can notice you drove off while the app is asleep and
	     ask whether you are still working. Nothing is recorded in between, nothing after clock-out,
	     and the clock itself is never changed without a tap (GeofencePlugin.swift). -->
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>While you are clocked in, North keeps a circle around the job site so it can notice when you leave — even with the screen off — and ask whether you are still working. It does not track where you go, records nothing between arriving and leaving, and stops the moment you clock out.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>North uses your location to stamp where you clocked in, verify you're at the job site, get local weather for scheduling, and to take Tap to Pay on iPhone payments — Apple requires a location fix to accept a card.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>North uses the microphone when you talk to Nort, the in-app assistant, or dictate notes. The recording is sent to a speech service to turn it into text.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>North can save documents and photos it generates back to your library.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>North lets you attach photos from your library to jobs, inspections and receipts.</string>
	<!-- TWO THINGS THE SHELL MAY DO WITH THE SCREEN OFF (field feedback 10-13, item 4). `audio`: Nort keeps
	     listening and talking after the screen locks (NortAudioPlugin.swift holds a real audio session;
	     the WebView's own capture cannot keep the app alive). `location`: the job's fence
	     (GeofencePlugin.swift) can wake the app when you drive off. Neither runs unless you started it:
	     a conversation with Nort, or a clock-in. -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>audio</string>
		<string>location</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
		<key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
		<dict>
			<key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
			<array>
				<dict>
					<key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
					<string>Default Configuration</string>
					<key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
					<string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).SceneDelegate</string>
					<key>UISceneStoryboardFile</key>
					<string>Main</string>
				</dict>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</dict>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>armv7</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIStatusBarStyle</key>
	<string>UIStatusBarStyleDarkContent</string>
	<!-- THE iPHONE'S INTERFACE NEVER ROTATES. PORTRAIT, FULL STOP — and that is the whole mechanism,
	     not a limitation of it.

	     Erik, on cn-v1041, which let iOS rotate: "nice it rotates now on schedule but the dock and top
	     bar rotate with it still." Of course they did: iOS rotating the interface is what carries them
	     around. What he asked for, twice, is the opposite — "lock the top bar and the dock positions
	     and just rotate the buttons while the internal screen rotates". The only way the top bar can
	     stay against the phone's top edge and the dock against its bottom edge is for nothing to move
	     at all, which is what this list now guarantees.

	     iOS still TELLS us which way the phone is physically held while the interface is locked
	     (UIDevice.orientation and the device-orientation notification), and ScreenTurnPlugin.swift
	     passes that one word to the page, which draws the region between the chrome a quarter turn.
	     WHICH screens may do that is still one declared list in the web app,
	     src/lib/screens-that-turn.ts, with the reason for every entry.

	     Changing this file is why the shell has to be rebuilt and reinstalled for any of it. -->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
